From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Mon, 28 Sep 2026 23:30:44 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 118/118] Scan: a tuner, tempo meter and key finder on the
 audio input (Settings > Tuning > Scan)

The Scan view (gui/views/scan_view.*, opened from Song view as the drone view is) listens to the internal
microphone, or to what is plugged into the mic or line input, and shows the note with its cents against the master
tune (so at 432 Hz too), its frequency, the tempo in BPM and the key, major or minor, with its Camelot number. The
select encoder chooses the input (Auto, Left, Right, Stereo; Auto: the internal microphone's left channel, stereo
with a jack plugged in; another input starts the analysis anew), pressed what is big on the OLED (note, Hz, BPM,
key); the horizontal encoder halves or doubles the BPM. The pads are the needle (a column per 6.7 cents, the middle
two green within 2 cents), the sidebar the input's level; the 7-segment display shows note, Hz, BPM or key (AMIN,
C#MAJ).

dsp/scan (deluge::dsp::Scan): the audio routine feeds it the input frames the recorders get (AudioEngine::startScan(),
feedScan() after i2sRXBufferPos moves), mixed and halved to 22.05 kHz by a half-band filter; the UI's routine
analyses them, one of the dearer parts per call (pitch and key take turns by how long each has waited, so neither
starves where the UI comes seldom). Pitch every 93 ms: YIN at 11.025 kHz (30 Hz to 2.2 kHz, the first dip whose
parabola's bottom is under 0.15), then the period measured again at 22.05 kHz over 1, 2, 4 ... periods up to 10 ms.
Tempo every second: the spectral flux (512 points every 11.6 ms), its autocorrelation over 8 s at the lags a beat
needs (the lag, its double and quadruple, its half), weighted towards 120 BPM, 60 to 207 BPM, nothing without 4 clear
onsets. Key every 0.5 s: the input at 5.5 kHz (a 31-tap low-pass as it comes), a 2048-point FFT, the peaks standing
three times above the bins around them (a note's partials: drums and noise have next to none, and a frame counts
where they make 1 % of the band) into 12 pitch classes against the master tune, summed over about 10 s, and the
major or minor key whose profile (Temperley's, with the notes' first four harmonics added) correlates best; nothing
unless it matches with 0.5 and 3 pitch classes are strong (a held note is no key), gone after 10 s without notes.
The object (95 kB) is allocated while the view is open; closed, nothing runs.

tests/scan (PC and Cortex-A9): tones B0 to C7 at 440 and 432 Hz within 0.2 cents (noise -15 dB: 1.9), the card's
20 multisamples on their note, 8 of 9 drum loops within 0.1 BPM (drum and bass at half), nothing for silence, noise
and a held tone; the key of I-vi-IV-V and i-VI-iv-V in all 24 keys played from the card's oboe and double bass, at
440 and 432 Hz, with a house loop as loud mixed in, and analysed only every 116 ms: 24 of 24 each; no key for
silence, noise, drums or a held note. Cost on the Cortex-A9: 4,773 instructions per 128 input frames; the analysis
about 2.1 % of the CPU while the view is open, its dearest call about 260,000 instructions (a pitch frame 251,000, a
chroma frame 241,000, a tempo estimate 173,000). tests/scan/scan_view_emu.py drives the view on the whole firmware in
the emulator (28 checks, the key of a chord in the input ring among them).

